## SIGKILL attribution for the agent's audit fallback (agent#275).
##
## The audit fallback sensor cannot see a SIGKILL aimed at the agent live: the agent dies
## before it reads the record. With this rule loaded, auditd writes one SYSCALL record per
## SIGKILL to /var/log/audit/audit.log; the restarted agent finds the one aimed at its
## predecessor and reports the sender (see docs/sensors/linux/audit-fallback.md).
##
## The packages neither install nor load it: it changes the host's audit configuration, which the
## agent never does by itself. Copy it from the repository deliberately:
##   cp synthaea-kill.rules /etc/audit/rules.d/ && augenrules --load
## It cannot be loaded when the audit configuration is immutable (-e 2). Only SIGKILL
## is matched (a1=9 for kill and tkill, a2=9 for tgkill), so `kill -0` and ordinary SIGTERM do
## not write a record. The volume of SIGKILLs on a real host is not measured. The agent reads a
## tkill or tgkill when the process id it names (tgkill's tgid, tkill's thread id) is the agent's
## pid or the id of one of its threads, which the agent lists in its marker file: SIGKILL is
## process-wide, so that kills it. A thread started in the seconds before the kill may be missing.
## A `systemctl kill`, rt_sigqueueinfo, rt_tgsigqueueinfo and pidfd_send_signal leave no record
## with these syscalls and stay unattributed.
## Needs no `-a never,task` ahead of it: a default Fedora ships one, which takes syscall
## auditing away from every task, and then nothing is recorded, not even a plain `kill -9`.
##
## The 64-bit lines are the same on x86_64 and aarch64 (`arch=b64`); the agent reads the syscall
## numbers of both (62/200/234 and 129/130/131). The `arch=b32` lines record the 32-bit entry of a
## 64-bit kernel (`int 0x80` from a 64-bit binary, i386 or arm compat; 37/238/270 and 37/238/268):
## IA32 emulation is on by default on Fedora, and without them that kill leaves no record. A kernel
## booted with `ia32_emulation=0` has no such entry and needs no `b32` line. Only x86_64 `b64` was
## captured; the `b32` lines were not loaded on a lab host.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S kill -F a1=9 -k synthaea-kill
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S tgkill -F a2=9 -k synthaea-kill
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S tkill -F a1=9 -k synthaea-kill
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S kill -F a1=9 -k synthaea-kill
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S tgkill -F a2=9 -k synthaea-kill
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S tkill -F a1=9 -k synthaea-kill
